52-page anthology, with a forward by Michiel van Ratingen, Secretary General, Euro NCAP, is available for purchase 【93†here.】\nL413: The book, contributed by leading thinkers on autonomy and safety, in addition to keen observers of the tech and car industries, asks the critical question about all the advancements in 【94†automotive sensors,】 software, hardware, next-generation vehicle architecture: How close are we, really, to making cars see and think for themselves?\nL414: \nL415: What needs to be developed? What issues are still up for debates? What pieces of this puzzle must still be tested and verified?\nL416: Contributors include: safety expert Phil Koopman, co-founder and CTO at Edge Case Research and associate professor, Carnegie Mellon University; Phil Magney, co-founder of VSI Labs whose firm is engaged in applied research on AV technologies; Colin Barnden, lead analyst at Semicast Research and a “Seriously Skeptical” columnist for EE Times;  Mike Demler, senior analyst at The Linley Group; Mark Fitzgerald, associate director for automotive at Strategy Analytics; Egil Juliussen, former director research at IHS Automotive, now the “Egil’s Eye” columnist for EE Times, and Rob Stead, conference director of AutoSens.\nL417: Partner Content\nL418: \nL419: 【95†View All 】\nL420: \nL421: 【160†Image: Breakthrough CNT Pellicles Deliver 66x Durability and Sufficient Transmittance】\nL422: \nL423: 【96†Breakthrough CNT Pellicles Deliver 66x Durability and Sufficient Transmittance】\nL424: \nL425: By Lintec  07.06.2026\nL426: \nL427: 【161†Image: Rapid Component Obsolescence Is Reshaping Today’s Semiconductor Procurement Dynamics】\nL428: \nL429: 【97†Rapid Component Obsolescence Is Reshaping Today’s Semiconductor Procurement Dynamics】\nL430: By Landyn Murphy, Senior Content Marketing Specialist, Rochester Electronics  07.01.2026\nL431: \nL432: 【162†Image: Sales Forecasting Guide for Electronics Manufacturing SMBs】\nL433: \nL434: 【98†Sales Forecasting Guide for Electronics Manufacturing SMBs】\nL435: \nL436: By MRPeasy  07.01.2026\nL437: \nL438: We put together the market overview on “connected, autonomous, shared and electric” vehicles, based on the data and insights provided by Yole Développement.\nL439: The book also includes our exclusive one-on-one interviews – on “The Chasm Between ADAS and AV” with Amnon Shashua, CEO of Mobileye, an Intel company, and another with Peter Hartwell, CTO of TDKInvenSense on “Augmenting Human Sensors in a car.”\nL440: \nL441: 【163†Image: sensors in automotive】Why the book?\nL442: \nL443: Many potential readers might ask: “Why the book now?”\nL444: We believe that every stakeholder, software/hardware developer, technology/business management and entrepreneur need right now a thoughtful book to prompt them to reflect, digest and explore all the options inherent to the development of highly automated vehicles.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhy a Book on ‘Sensors in Automotive’? - EE Times (https://www.eetimes.com/why-a-book-on-sensors-in-automotive/)\nciteturn18view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view1\",\"pattern\":\"store\"}); Total lines: 688\nL514: 【115†Pressure and Ultrasonic Flow Sensing for Smarter Fluid Systems】\nL515: \nL516: 【116†The New Software Standard for Physical AI】\nL517: \nL518: 【117†Reliable Machine Vision Starts at the Circuit Level】\nL519: \nL520: 【118†The First-Time-Right Revolution】\nL521: \nL522: Related Articles\nL523: \nL524: 【165†Image】\nL525: \nL526: 【41† design Lines AI & Big Data Designline 】\nL527: # Why a Book on ‘Sensors in Automotive’?\nL528: \nL529: ## It’s complicated\nL530: \nL531: By 【119†Junko Yoshida】  10.20.2020 0\nL532: ### Share this:\nL533: \nL534:   * 【90† Share on X (Opens in new window) X 】\nL535:   * 【91† Share on Facebook (Opens in new window) Facebook 】\nL536:   * 【92† Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window) LinkedIn 】\nL537:   *\nL538: \nL539: We are releasing a new book, “ASPENCORE Guide to: Sensors in Automotive — Making Cars See and Think Ahead” on Tuesday, October 20th.\nL540: EE Times and its publisher, AspenCore Media, have been chronicling the efforts to build a truly safe driverless car since the first sensor technologies emerged that made the task seem doable.\nL541: As we’ve reported on the developments in sensors, software, hardware, and next-gen vehicle architecture, the question at the center of our investigations has always been: How close are we, really, to making cars see and think for themselves? It’s a critical topic whose complexities could fill a book — so we’ve produced one.\nL542: 【166†Image†www.eetimes.com】\nL543: \nL544: With a forward by 【120†Euro NCAP †www.euroncap.com】(The European New Car Assessment Programme) Secretary General Michiel van Ratingen, “AspenCore’s Guide to Sensors in Automotive” is available for purchase now at【121† EE Times’ Book Store.†eetimes.com】\nL545: \nL546: Partner Content\nL547: \nL548: 【95†View All 】\nL549: \nL550: 【167†Image: Breakthrough CNT Pellicles Deliver 66x Durability and Sufficient Transmittance】\nL551: \nL552: 【96†Breakthrough CNT Pellicles Deliver 66x Durability and Sufficient Transmittance】\nL553: By Lintec  07.06.2026\nL554: \nL555: 【168†Image: Rapid Component Obsolescence Is Reshaping Today’s Semiconductor Procurement Dynamics】\nL556: \nL557: 【97†Rapid Component Obsolescence Is Reshaping Today’s Semiconductor Procurement Dynamics】\nL558: \nL559: By Landyn Murphy, Senior Content Marketing Specialist, Rochester Electronics  07.01.2026\nL560: \nL561: 【169†Image: Sales Forecasting Guide for Electronics Manufacturing SMBs】\nL562: \nL563: 【98†Sales Forecasting Guide for Electronics Manufacturing SMBs】\nL564: \nL565: By MRPeasy  07.01.2026\nL566: \nL567: * * *\nL568: To Purchase this book, please go to EE Times Store.\nL569: 【122† Sensors in Automotive – Making Cars See and Think Ahead †eetimes.vipdev.lndo.site】\nL570: \nL571: * * *\nL572: The secretary general is not the only luminary with a byline in the book; leading thinkers on autonomy and safety, along with keen observers of the tech and car industries, participated in this project. Collectively, they ask — and, from the vantage point of their expertise, answer — not just how close we are to true autonomy but how, and even whether, we get there. What needs to be developed? What issues are still up for debate? What pieces of this puzzle must still be tested and verified?\nL573: 【170†Image†www.eetimes.com】\nL574: Our 152-page anthology offers contributions from industry safety expert Phil Koopman, co-founder and CTO at Edge Case Research and an associate professor at Carnegie Mellon University; Phil Magney, co-founder of VSI Labs, which is engaged in applied research on AV technologies; Colin Barnden, lead analyst at Semicast Research and “Seriously Skeptical” columnist for EE Times;  Mike Demler, senior analyst at The Linley Group; Mark Fitzgerald, associate director for automotive at Strategy Analytics; Egil Juliussen, former director research at IHS Automotive and “Egil’s Eye” columnist for EE Times, and Rob Stead, conference director of AutoSens.\nL575: 【171†Image†www.eetimes.com】\nL576: We also put together a market overview on “connected, autonomous, shared and electric” vehicles, based on data and insights provided by Yole Développement.\nL577: You’ll find exclusive one-on-one interviews as well: Amnon Shashua, CEO of Intel’s Mobileye spoke with us about “The Chasm Between ADAS and AV,” and Peter Hartwell, CTO of TDK InvenSense, shared his perspectives on “Augmenting Human Senses in Cars.” And we compiled a References section of great tech papers from Intel, Nvidia, Siemens, NXP, Arm, Omnivision, and Qualcomm.\nL578: Why a book?\nL579: As we’ve said, it’s a complex topic. So we crafted this book as a resource for the stakeholders, software/hardware developers, technology/business executives, and entrepreneurs who must explore, reflect on, and understand all the options and decisions inherent in the development highly automated vehicles.\nL580: EE Times has done extensive reporting on new products and technologies, covered all the business and technology alliances, and written about the design wins and the horse races among technology suppliers. This coverage conveys the excitement and occasionally indulges in the hype about how ADAS and autonomous vehicles are going to save lives. We’ve done our homework, and this book is the result.\nL581: \nL582: But the technology is still waiting to be graded.\nL583: The very idea of putting together a book struck us as bold — and perhaps even an overreach — when Victor Gao, CMO of AspenCore’s parent Arrow, first pitched it. He countered our hesitancy by reminding us of the value in our day-to-day work. Reporters follow the news from a front-row seat, asking questions and educating readers on fast-changing technology and industry scuttlebutt. For us, it’s a grind. But Victor reminded us that, when considered all together, this harvest yields a bounty of insight.\nL584: Anne-Françoise Pelé, EE Times’ French correspondent and AspenCore’s book editor, articulated the vision when she noted, “We reporters, always looking for the next big story, tend to forget that we are also writing history as news breaks and events unfold.” She saw our book as standing at the nexus of the past (what we have reported) and the future (what we will report), and that observation gave the project shape.\nL585: When we mined ApsenCore’s collective coverage for scaffold on which to build the book, we found useful data points from different times and perspectives but needed to connect them coherently. I insisted that we delve deeper into the issues. We did so with the help of independent experts and industry analysts who were all willing to share their views and insights, candidly and impartially.\nL586: \nL587: We’re pleased with the result and believe you will be, too.\nL588: \nL589: A Quote from the Foreword\nL609: — Bettina Weiss, Chief of Staff & Global Smart Mobility Lead, SEMI\nL610: \nL611: 【178†Image†www.eetimes.com】In Sensors in Automotive, Junko Yoshida and her team have done a masterful job of assembling insights and perspectives from many of the people best positioned to assess the potential and the challenges of automotive perception technologies.\nL612: \nL613: — Jeff Bier, Founder, Edge AI and Vision Alliance | President, BDTI\nL614: \nL615: 【179†Image†www.eetimes.com】\nL616: With autonomous, connected, electrified, and shared (ACES) vehicles predicted to have over double the sensor content of traditional cars, the AspenCore Guide to Sensors in Automotive is an important publication for engineers in both the sensors and transportation sectors. The updates on sensor technology, safety standards, and the automated vehicle design itself provide useful insight on the path to solving the self-driving challenge.\nL617: — Tim Brosnihan, Executive Director MEMS & Sensors Industry Group, SEMI\nL618: \nL619: 【180†Image†www.eetimes.com】The book offers easy-to-read insights and brings pedagogy and down-to-earth questioning to a world often constrained by pure marketing or engineering thinking.\nL620: The Automotive industry is currently at a crossroad, safety and autonomy being one aspect, connectivity and carbon neutrality the other. While these trends are currently reshaping the balance of power between the automotive players, the impact on the semiconductor industry is important to understand.\nL621: Cars are morphing into robots, and the importance for the semiconductor players, whether they are  the “More than Moore” players supplying sensors or the “More Moore” players supplying the associated computing, is to get a slice of the $2,400 billion car sales market as of 2019.\nL622: \nL623: Wrapping up the full story in a single book is a tremendous idea from Aspencore. And I am immensely proud of the numerous contributions from Yole  Développement analysts it contains.\nL624: — Pierre Cambou, Principal Analyst at Yole Développement\nL625: \nL626: * * *\nL627: \nL628: To Purchase this book, please go to EE Times Store.\nL629: 【122† Sensors in Automotive – Making Cars See and Think Ahead †eetimes.vipdev.lndo.site】\nL630: \nL631: * * *\nL632: ### Share this:\nL633: \nL634:   * 【90† Share on X (Opens in new window) X 】\nL635:   * 【91† Share on Facebook (Opens in new window) Facebook 】\nL636:   * 【92† Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window) LinkedIn 】\nL637:   *\nL638: \nL639: 【181†Image†www.eetimes.com】\nL640: \nL641: 【119†Junko Yoshida】\nL642: Junko Yoshida is a multiple award-winning journalist who most recently served as Global Editor-in-Chief for Aspencore. Aspencore owns EE Times, a publication for whom she was a reporter, editor and manager for more than thirty years. Now retired from EE Times, she continues to report and comment on innovations, business and issues in high technology. 【123†Follow Junko on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com】\nL643: \nL644: 0 comments\nL645: \nL646: 【32†Post Comment 】\nL647: ### Leave a Reply 【106†Cancel reply】\nL648: \nL649: You must 【32†Register】 or 【32†Login】 to post a comment.\nL650: \nL651: This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. 【107†Learn how your comment data is processed.†akismet.com】\nL652: \nL653: Related Articles\nL654: \nL655: 【182†Image】\nL656: \nL657: Aspencore Network\nL658:   * 【124† EE Times 】\nL659:   * 【125† EE Times Asia †www.eetasia.com】\nL660:   * 【126† EE Times China †www.eet-china.com】\nL661:   * 【127† EE Times India †www.eetindia.com】\nL662:   * 【128† EE Times Taiwan †www.eettaiwan.com】\nL663:   * 【129† EE Times Japan †eetimes.itmedia.co.jp】\nL664:   * 【130† EDN †www.edn.com】\nL665:   * 【131† EDN Asia †www.ednasia.com】\nL666:   * 【132† EDN Taiwan †www.edntaiwan.com】\nL667:   * 【133† ESM China †www.esm-china.com】\nL668:   * 【134† EDN China †www.ednchina.com】\nL669:   * 【135† EDN Japan †edn.itmedia.co.jp】\nL670:   * 【136† Power Electronics News †www.powere